1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Right now, the phone in your pocket is quietly

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,400
syncing its time with atomic clocks —

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,000
machines so precise they would barely drift a second

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,600
in millions of years.

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,300
But for most of human history,

6
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,100
nobody knew exactly what time it was.

7
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:19,100
And honestly? Nobody really cared.

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:23,000
So how did we go from watching shadows on

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,600
the ground, to splitting a single second into billions

10
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:27,400
of pieces?

11
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,700
Let's rewind. Way back.

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,400
For early humans, time was simple.

13
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:36,400
The sun came up —

14
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,600
you got up. The sun went down —

15
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,200
you went to sleep.

16
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,900
Seasons mattered way more than minutes.

17
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:45,900
When will the river flood?

18
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,900
When do the animals move?

19
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:49,500
When should we plant?

20
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:51,800
So people watched the sky.

21
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:55,400
Monuments like Stonehenge line up with the sun on

22
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:57,000
the solstices — basically,

23
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,200
giant stone calendars.

24
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,700
But to track time within a single day,

25
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,300
you needed something simpler:

26
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,100
a stick. Plant it in the ground,

27
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,700
and its shadow moves as the sun crosses the

28
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:10,100
sky.

29
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:14,000
The ancient Egyptians turned this idea into real tools.

30
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,200
Around three and a half thousand years ago,

31
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,800
they were using shadow clocks and sundials to split

32
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,000
daylight into parts.

33
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,100
They divided the day into twelve hours,

34
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:27,500
and the night into another twelve.

35
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,700
That's where our twenty-four hour day comes from.

36
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,600
But here's the twist:

37
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:34,200
those hours weren't equal.

38
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:37,000
Daylight was always split into twelve parts,

39
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,600
so a summer hour was longer than a winter

40
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:41,000
hour.

41
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:44,900
Imagine your work shift getting longer every single summer.

42
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:45,700
No thanks.

43
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,400
And sundials had one huge bug.

44
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,400
They don't work at night.

45
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:52,000
Or when it's cloudy.

46
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:55,700
So the Egyptians tried something else:

47
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:57,300
water. A water clock,

48
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:00,900
or clepsydra, is basically a stone bowl with a

49
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,900
tiny hole near the bottom.

50
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:06,000
Water drips out at a steady rate,

51
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,600
and marks on the inside tell you how much

52
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:13,200
time has passed. One surviving Egyptian water clock is

53
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:16,000
around three thousand four hundred years old.

54
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:19,900
The ancient Greeks even used water clocks in court,

55
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,700
to limit how long people could speak.

56
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,500
Talk too long? Your water runs out.

57
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,400
Meanwhile, in Mesopotamia, the Babylonians were obsessed with the

58
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:33,000
number sixty. They did their math in base sixty.

59
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:36,500
Why sixty? Because it splits evenly into two,

60
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:38,100
three, four, five, six,

61
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:39,700
ten, twelve, fifteen, twenty,

62
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:42,900
and thirty. Super handy for dividing things up.

63
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,400
That ancient habit, passed down through Greek astronomers,

64
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:50,000
is why an hour has sixty minutes and a

65
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,600
minute has sixty seconds.

66
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,000
Every time you check the clock,

67
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,600
you're doing Babylonian math.

68
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:58,500
Fast forward to China,

69
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:02,100
in the 1090s. A scholar named Su Song built

70
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,900
something incredible: a giant astronomical clock tower,

71
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:06,900
more than ten meters tall.

72
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,000
It was powered by a water wheel,

73
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,600
and it used a clever mechanism called an escapement,

74
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:16,400
letting the wheel move forward in small,

75
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:17,200
regular steps.

76
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,500
It rang bells, struck gongs,

77
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:22,300
and rotated a model of the stars.

78
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:25,500
A water-powered machine, almost a thousand years ago.

79
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:29,400
In Europe, the push for better clocks came from

80
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:33,000
monks. Monasteries held prayers at set times of the

81
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,600
day and night, and someone had to ring the

82
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:37,800
bell on time.

83
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:41,700
In fact, the word clock comes from the medieval

84
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:43,300
Latin word clocca —

85
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:44,100
meaning bell.

86
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,400
By the late twelve hundreds,

87
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,800
Europeans were building fully mechanical clocks,

88
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:52,400
driven by heavy falling weights and controlled by an

89
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:52,800
escapement.

90
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:54,700
These clocks were huge,

91
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,100
loud, and honestly, not that accurate.

92
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:00,700
They could drift by many minutes every single day.

93
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,800
Many didn't even have a minute hand.

94
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,600
Just one hand, pointing to the hour.

95
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:07,800
Because why bother?

96
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:10,900
Still, towns proudly put them in towers.

97
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:14,100
Now the whole town heard the same hours,

98
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:16,500
ringing out from the same tower.

99
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:21,400
Then came a bored young man in a church.

100
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:24,200
The story goes that in the 1580s,

101
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,800
a student named Galileo Galilei watched a lamp swinging

102
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:29,000
in Pisa Cathedral.

103
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:30,900
He noticed something weird:

104
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:34,500
big swings and small swings seemed to take the

105
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,100
same amount of time.

106
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:39,600
Historians aren't sure that lamp story really happened.

107
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:42,000
But Galileo really did study pendulums,

108
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,400
and realized they could keep time.

109
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:48,300
In 1656, the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens built the

110
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:49,500
first pendulum clock.

111
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:53,400
The result? Clocks went from losing minutes per day,

112
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:55,000
to losing only seconds.

113
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,200
Suddenly, a minute hand was actually worth adding.

114
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:01,700
For the next two hundred and seventy years,

115
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:04,500
the pendulum ruled the world of timekeeping.

116
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:08,200
But pendulums have one big weakness:

117
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,600
put one on a rocking ship,

118
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:12,200
and it goes crazy.

119
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:15,700
And at sea, that was a deadly problem.

120
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:18,500
Sailors could work out their latitude —

121
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:21,700
how far north or south they were —

122
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:24,100
from the sun and the stars.

123
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:28,000
But longitude — how far east or west —

124
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,400
was a nightmare. Ships got lost,

125
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,000
ran out of supplies,

126
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:33,600
or crashed into rocks.

127
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:37,500
In 1707, four British warships wrecked off the Isles

128
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:41,100
of Scilly, and well over a thousand sailors died.

129
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:45,000
Here's the trick. The Earth turns fifteen degrees every

130
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,600
hour. So if you know the time back home,

131
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:51,400
and the local time where you are,

132
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,800
the difference tells you your longitude.

133
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:56,100
Local time is easy —

134
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:57,700
just watch the sun.

135
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:00,500
But keeping home time on a rocking,

136
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:04,100
freezing, salty ship? That needed a clock no one

137
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:05,300
had built yet.

138
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:09,200
In 1714, the British government offered a prize worth

139
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:12,800
millions in today's money to anyone who could solve

140
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:13,200
it.

141
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:17,100
A self-taught carpenter named John Harrison spent decades on

142
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:18,700
the problem. His masterpiece,

143
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:21,900
the H4, looked like a big pocket watch.

144
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:25,400
On a test voyage to Jamaica in 1761,

145
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:29,000
it lost only about five seconds in eighty-one days

146
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:29,800
at sea.

147
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:33,100
Navigation was never the same again.

148
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:36,400
Now, even in the 1800s,

149
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:40,000
every town still set its clocks by its own

150
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,600
sun. Noon was whenever the sun was highest —

151
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:44,800
right there, locally.

152
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:48,700
So noon in one city might be several minutes

153
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,900
different from noon in the next city over.

154
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:53,500
No big deal —

155
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:55,100
until trains showed up.

156
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:59,000
Railways needed schedules. And schedules with dozens of different

157
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,400
local times were a total mess.

158
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,300
Britain fixed it first.

159
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:04,900
By the late 1840s,

160
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:08,100
most British railways were running on London time.

161
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:10,800
In North America, on November eighteenth,

162
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:14,000
1883, the railroads switched to standard time zones.

163
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,200
People called it the Day of Two Noons,

164
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:20,800
because many towns saw noon twice as clocks were

165
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:21,200
reset.

166
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:23,500
A year later, in 1884,

167
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:26,300
delegates from twenty-five countries met in Washington,

168
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:28,300
and chose Greenwich, in London,

169
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:29,900
as the prime meridian:

170
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:31,100
zero degrees longitude.

171
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:33,114
Time stopped being local.

172
00:07:33,114 --> 00:07:34,400
It became global.

173
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:39,300
In 1927, engineers at Bell Labs built the first

174
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:40,100
quartz clock.

175
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:43,200
Quartz is a crystal with a superpower.

176
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:45,600
Squeeze it, and it makes electricity.

177
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:47,200
Zap it with electricity,

178
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:48,800
and it vibrates —

179
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:50,800
at a very steady rate.

180
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:54,300
Count those vibrations, and you've got a clock.

181
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:57,900
A typical quartz watch crystal vibrates exactly thirty-two thousand,

182
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:00,700
seven hundred and sixty-eight times per second.

183
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,400
In 1969, Seiko released the Astron,

184
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,400
the world's first quartz wristwatch.

185
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:07,400
Within a couple of decades,

186
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:10,200
cheap and accurate quartz watches were everywhere.

187
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,500
But scientists wanted even better.

188
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,900
So they looked inside the atom.

189
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:18,800
In 1955, Louis Essen and Jack Parry in Britain

190
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:21,200
built the first accurate atomic clock,

191
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:22,400
using cesium atoms.

192
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:26,300
Atoms of the same type vibrate at exactly the

193
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:27,900
same frequency. Always. Everywhere.

194
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:29,500
They're nature's perfect metronome.

195
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:33,000
In 1967, the world officially redefined the second.

196
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,600
One second is now the time it takes for

197
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:39,000
nine billion, one hundred ninety-two million,

198
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,600
six hundred thirty-one thousand,

199
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:44,200
seven hundred and seventy cycles of radiation from a

200
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:45,000
cesium atom.

201
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:48,900
Yep. The second is no longer based on the

202
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:51,300
Earth's spin. It's based on atoms.

203
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:55,800
And you use atomic clocks every single day,

204
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:57,400
without even knowing it.

205
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,600
Every GPS satellite carries atomic clocks on board.

206
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:04,500
Your phone figures out where you are by measuring

207
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:08,100
how long signals take to arrive from several satellites.

208
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:12,000
Light travels about thirty centimeters in a billionth of

209
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,600
a second. So a timing error of just one

210
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:19,200
millionth of a second could put you around three

211
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:20,400
hundred meters off.

212
00:09:20,700 --> 00:09:24,300
Today, the best experimental optical clocks are so precise,

213
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:27,900
they wouldn't lose a second over the entire age

214
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:29,100
of the universe.

215
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:33,000
Fun fact: the Earth itself is a sloppy clock.

216
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Its spin is slightly irregular,

217
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,600
so since 1972, scientists have added leap seconds to

218
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,800
keep atomic time in sync with the planet.

219
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:44,900
But leap seconds cause headaches for computers.

220
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:48,500
So in 2022, the world voted to scrap them

221
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:49,300
by 2035.

222
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:53,400
So, from a stick in the ground,

223
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,800
to a crystal on your wrist,

224
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:59,400
to atoms vibrating billions of times a second —

225
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:03,300
humans spent thousands of years chasing one simple question:

226
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:04,900
what time is it?

227
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:07,600
And the funny thing is —

228
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,800
now that we can measure time almost perfectly,

229
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:14,400
we still never seem to have enough of it.

230
00:10:14,700 --> 00:10:17,500
If you enjoyed this trip through time,

231
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:21,100
subscribe. Next time, we'll find out how humans went

232
00:10:21,100 --> 00:10:23,500
from trading cows to using money.

233
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:24,700
See you then!
